  <div class="abstract">
    <p data-label="Summary">The Report tab sets the measures of a workflow out as a short scientific report: the
    shooting details, the star size, signal and sky level of each frame, the figures each block measured, the settings
    that were changed. It computes nothing of its own. Every number is a fact a block already wrote, and the report
    names the block it comes from. It is read in the tab as a PDF, exported as PDF or PNG, and its parts can be copied
    as text or as pictures for a forum.</p>
  </div>

  <ol class="chain" aria-label="Position in the graph">
    <li><span>Stars/background</span></li><li><span>Post</span></li>
    <li class="here"><span>Report</span></li>
  </ol>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s1"><span class="secnum">1</span>What the report is for</h2>
    <aside class="note"><b>No path, no folder name.</b> A setting whose value names a place on a disk is left out, so
    that a report can be shared as it is.</aside>
    <p>A processed picture does not say what was shot, what the sky was like that night, or what was done to get it.
    The report is that record: what was shot, what was measured, what was done. It is made to be kept with the project
    or posted with the picture.</p>
    <p>It is a document, not a dashboard: a white page, numbered sections, tables with thin rules, black figures with
    their captions. The tables are the blocks’ own facts, the same ones Inspect shows. The figures are drawn for the
    report; Inspect keeps its interactive charts.</p>
    <p>The tab has two parts. On the left, a form for what the files do not say, and the buttons that copy. On the
    right, the report itself in a PDF reader: what is seen is what is exported. It zooms with − and +, with Ctrl and the
    mouse wheel, and Fit width returns to the page width. When the project holds several workflows, a list above the
    form chooses which one the report describes.</p>
    <p>Until the workflow has been run, the report says so and shows nothing else. If a block is outdated (a night was
    added since the last run), the report is made from the results it had and says so in red italics, instead of
    claiming nothing is computed.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s2"><span class="secnum">2</span>The first page</h2>
    <aside class="note"><b>Why the date is typed.</b> A camera’s clock may be wrong, so the date is never read from the
    files. It appears only when it was entered.</aside>
    <p>The first page opens like a journal article. It holds the target’s name (the project’s target, else its name),
    the name of the workflow and of the <a href="/blocks/post/">Post</a> version when there are several, and one line of position: the centre of
    the field in right ascension and declination (ICRS), the size of the field and the scale in arcseconds per pixel,
    all three from the <a href="/blocks/astrometry/">Astrometry</a> block. Then comes the final image, as Post last
    showed it (figure 1 of the report), and one small line with the program’s name, “Processing report” and the date.</p>
    <p>Two tables follow.</p>
    <p><strong>Table 1, Acquisition</strong> lists the shooting details in the order of a forum post. Each row appears
    only when known.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Camera, with its Gain or ISO speed. Both come from the frames’ headers; Gain is written when the header names
      a gain (an astronomy camera) and ISO otherwise.</li>
      <li>Instrument, Mount, Filter, Location and Date. The files do not say them: they are typed in the form on the
      left of the tab, kept in the project, and the Project block’s form edits the same values. Undo takes an entry
      back.</li>
      <li>Frames: the frames kept, grouped by exposure (for example 90 × 180 s), with their total exposure to the
      second. Darks, Flats and Bias: their numbers.</li>
      <li>Sampling, the scale of the final pixels, when it is not already the one of the line under the title.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The focal length, the star size and the field are left out of this table when they are given elsewhere on the
    page.</p>
    <p><strong>Table 2, Figures measured on the frames of this session</strong> gives, for each figure, its value
    and how it was measured. A figure is listed only when the block that measures it has run. Table 1 of this page
    lists them.</p>

    <div class="tbl-wrap wide">
      <table class="tbl">
        <caption><b>Table 1.</b>The figures of the head of the report, and the block each comes from.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Figure</th><th>Value</th><th>How it is measured</th><th>Block</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Focal length, measured</th><td>in mm</td><td>From the solved sky: the number of stars used and the residual of the fit, in arcseconds.</td><td>Astrometry</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Star FWHM, measured</th><td>median, in arcseconds</td><td>Median star width, Moffat fit, over the frames kept, with the best frame. Given in pixels when the scale on the sky is not known.</td><td><a href="/blocks/registration/">Registration</a></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Field drift</th><td>in arcseconds</td><td>How far the field moved on the sensor during the session (tracking and deliberate shifts), with the rate per minute when the first and last times are known.</td><td>Registration</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Colours offset, corrected</th><td>in pixels</td><td>The larger of the red and blue offsets from green, with both, and the number of stars they were measured on. Listed only when the correction was applied.</td><td><a href="/blocks/stacking/">Stacking</a></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Sky noise</th><td>÷ n</td><td>The noise of the green channel before Denoise over the noise after it, each 1.4826 times the median absolute deviation of the linear image minus its Gaussian blur (σ 2 px).</td><td><a href="/blocks/denoise/">Denoise</a></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Sky gradient, removed</th><td>in %</td><td>The spread of the sky across the field against its level, modelled on a number of samples.</td><td><a href="/blocks/gradient/">Gradient</a></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Gaia DR3 stars in the field</th><td>a count</td><td>The stars of the catalogue in the field, and how many of them have their measured spectrum.</td><td>Catalogs (the stage the Color block reads)</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Sensor response, rebuilt</th><td>a count</td><td>The stars whose spectra rebuilt the sensor’s colour response (section 4).</td><td><a href="/blocks/color/">Color</a></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Transparency changes</th><td>in %</td><td>The signal of the stars from frame to frame: the gap between the tenth and the ninetieth percentile, over the ninetieth.</td><td>Registration</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s3"><span class="secnum">3</span>The night, frame by frame</h2>
    <aside class="note"><b>Minutes since the first frame.</b> The horizontal axis counts from the session’s first frame,
    never by the clock, which may be wrong. Without usable times it counts the frame numbers.</aside>
    <p>Each session of the workflow has one figure, with up to three panels on one horizontal axis. Every point is a
    frame.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>FWHM:</strong> the star size, from the Moffat fit, in arcseconds when the scale on the sky is known,
      else in pixels.</li>
      <li><strong>Relative signal (%):</strong> the signal of the stars in the frame relative to the best ones (100 is
      the best tenth, and a frame can go a little above).</li>
      <li><strong>Sky level (ADU):</strong> the sky level of the frame after calibration. The panel is drawn only when
      the calibration’s statistics are there.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The frames kept are black dots. The frames left out are open grey circles. The size, the signal and the times
    come from the <a href="/blocks/registration/">Registration</a> block, the sky level from <a href="/blocks/calibration/">Calibration</a>. The frames
    left out are the ones of the <a href="/blocks/selection/">Selection</a> block, or those excluded by hand.</p>
    <p>When the project has several sessions, each gets its own figure, titled with its name. A small table of the
    session’s figures goes with it: Frames kept, Total exposure, Median, Best and Worst FWHM (the median also in pixels
    when it is in arcseconds), and Median roundness. With a single session it is shown only if the roundness was
    measured.</p>
    <p>The same figure exists as a night card: the picture with a heading (“Night card”, the target, the session’s
    name when there are several) and a foot of the same figures, made to be posted with the final image. <a href="/blocks/post/">Post</a>’s Export
    writes it next to the exported picture, as a PNG, one per session. When the picture’s facts are known, the card also
    states what the whole picture is made of: its number of frames and total exposure, then the same for each session
    or workflow when there are several.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s4"><span class="secnum">4</span>One section per block</h2>
    <aside class="note"><b>Figures hidden.</b> A few figures of the blocks are not shown, because a reader has no use
    for them: the star size range, the cache size, the flat minimum, the integration, the stacked exposure and the drift
    in pixels. The star size of 2 × 2 super-pixels is also left out: it overestimates undersampled stars, and the
    report quotes the Moffat fit’s.</aside>
    <p>After the first page, the report has one numbered section for each block of the workflow, in the order of the
    chain: <a href="/blocks/calibration/">Calibration</a>, <a href="/blocks/registration/">Registration</a>, <a href="/blocks/selection/">Selection</a>, <a href="/blocks/stacking/">Stacking</a>, <a href="/blocks/astrometry/">Astrometry</a>, <a href="/blocks/gradient/">Gradient</a>, <a href="/blocks/denoise/">Denoise</a>, <a href="/blocks/color/">Color</a>, <a href="/blocks/stars/">Stars/background</a>.
    A block the workflow does not have has no section. Each section opens with one sentence on what the block does.
    If the block has not run, or is outdated, its state is written under that sentence.</p>
    <p>Then come the block’s figures, one row each, with the meaning of the figure beside it. These are the figures
    Inspect shows for the block, so a reader of the report does not need the manual. Two sections add a figure.</p>
    <h3>Registration: drift and residual</h3>
    <p>A figure of two panels against the frame number. The upper one gives the offset of each frame from the
    reference frame, in x (solid line) and in y (dashed), in arcseconds when the scale is known, else in pixels. The
    lower one gives the alignment residual of each frame, in pixels. The figure is left out when no offset was
    measured.</p>
    <h3>Color: approximate spectral response of the sensor</h3>
    <p>A figure of the red, green and blue curves of the sensor, relative to their maximum, against wavelength in
    nm. The positions of Hβ (486.1 nm), [OIII] (500.7 nm) and Hα (656.3 nm) are marked with dotted lines. Its caption
    gives the number of stars used.</p>
    <p>The curves are rebuilt from the Color block’s measure of its stars in the camera’s
    colours, and from the Gaia XP spectra of the same stars. A star’s flux in a channel is the integral of its
    spectrum times the channel’s curve, and each curve is a non-negative sum of smooth Gaussians (a centre every 15 nm
    from 380 to 1000 nm), with a penalty on its roughness, as in the recovery of camera sensitivities from known
    spectra.<a class="cite" href="#ref-1">[1]</a> The stars the fit cannot explain are rejected over four passes. The
    curves change no colour: they are a diagnostic. The computation takes about a second and nothing is stored. It needs
    at least 50 colour stars, and the catalogue’s spectra; without them the figure is left out.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s5"><span class="secnum">5</span>Recipe and appendix</h2>
    <p><strong>Recipe</strong> is the section after the last block. It writes the chain as a line, for example
    Calibration → Registration → Stacking, and below it a table of the settings that differ from the defaults, as
    “block · setting” and its value. When nothing was changed, it says that all the settings are the defaults.
    Values that are not plain (a number, a text, yes or no) and values that name a place on a disk are left out.</p>
    <p><strong>Appendix: settings</strong> follows, with one small table of settings per block. It lists the changed
    settings, and the unchanged ones except those of the advanced level, which change nothing a reader needs. Of the
    two stacking methods, it lists only the settings of the one that ran. The appendix pieces have no title of their
    own, so they are not offered as pictures to copy.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s6"><span class="secnum">6</span>Copy and export</h2>
    <p>Everything that leaves the tab is made from what the reader shows.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Copy text.</strong> The shooting details as plain text, shown in the box of the form: the target, then
      one line per known detail (“Camera: …”, “Mount: …”, “Frames: …”, “Processing: FLOAT”), nothing for what is
      unknown. A list chooses the language of this text, French or English, whatever the language of the screen.</li>
      <li><strong>Copy image.</strong> A list chooses the part, and the button puts it on the clipboard as a picture:
      the session card (the first page), the night cards, then each numbered section. Pictures are made at twice the
      page’s size.</li>
      <li><strong>Export PDF.</strong> The whole report, on A4 pages, with a foot on each page (the title, the
      program’s name, the page number). A piece that does not fit the rest of a page goes whole to the next one; none
      is cut.</li>
      <li><strong>Export PNG.</strong> The whole report as one tall picture.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Both exports propose a file named after the target, ending in <i>_report</i>, in the project’s folder. The
    status line says when the file is written. The figures use a decimal comma in French.</p>
  </section>

